WEBVTT

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:07.120
Welcome to G H U Lab. This revised
walkthrough covers every menu section,

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.720
the three Simulator modes and the embedded
research experiments.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.575
It now demonstrates the S U seven
certificates, approximation errors,
uncertainty

00:00:15.575 --> 00:00:19.600
budget, thermal solver comparison, named
collider references and the first

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.730
Kaluza Klein gluon against L H C data.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.035
Choose a chapter to revisit a topic.

00:00:26.335 --> 00:00:28.880
Start with a named model or reference.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.300
Change one assumption at a time, and read
the result with its units and scope.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.290
Theorem, verified, measured and unknown
describe different kinds of evidence.

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.460
A computed scenario value is not
automatically an experimental measurement.

00:00:45.602 --> 00:00:50.177
Open Hierarchy. For a bulk content of the S
U seven model, inspect the

00:00:50.177 --> 00:00:54.202
compactification scale and Higgs mass under
the displayed conventions.

00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:57.847
The ceiling belongs to the stated small-
angle moment approximation.

00:00:59.135 --> 00:01:02.655
Change the diagnostic gauge coupling to
zero point seven.

00:01:02.655 --> 00:01:06.080
The response plots separate a change of
model convention from propagation

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.390
of the measured W input and from winding
convergence.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.055
Read the axes and the central values
together.

00:01:13.402 --> 00:01:16.887
The calculated phase does not reproduce the
printed phase.

00:01:16.887 --> 00:01:20.707
The mismatch varies between rows, and an
overall multiplication of the potential

00:01:20.707 --> 00:01:23.177
cannot move its stationary points.

00:01:23.177 --> 00:01:27.037
Absolute scales remain conditional on
resolving this discrepancy.

00:01:27.037 --> 00:01:29.837
The arithmetic identities are separate
statements.

00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:33.560
Load the first certified benchmark.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.405
The table encloses two different stationary
points: the quartic logarithmic

00:01:37.405 --> 00:01:40.095
approximation and the full Fourier minimum.

00:01:40.095 --> 00:01:44.175
Phase, Higgs mass and compactification
scale have separate absolute and relative

00:01:44.175 --> 00:01:48.425
error intervals. They apply to the
displayed matter content, gauge choice

00:01:48.425 --> 00:01:50.185
and fixed mass inputs.

00:01:51.535 --> 00:01:53.415
What limits the precision?

00:01:53.415 --> 00:01:57.455
Read approximation bias, root-location
bounds and the Fourier remainder separately

00:01:57.455 --> 00:02:00.325
from the propagated W-mass uncertainty.

00:02:00.325 --> 00:02:04.085
The ten percent coupling variation is a
chosen scenario, without an assigned

00:02:04.085 --> 00:02:08.725
probability. Physical theory uncertainty is
explicitly not quantified;

00:02:08.725 --> 00:02:11.150
there is no combined physical error bar.

00:02:12.468 --> 00:02:14.488
Save the error certificate.

00:02:14.488 --> 00:02:18.548
The download keeps the rational enclosures,
numerical budget, W input and

00:02:18.548 --> 00:02:23.383
attribution. The full mass enclosure
already includes root and Fourier errors;

00:02:23.383 --> 00:02:25.553
do not add its half-width again.

00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:28.693
A different matter content or mass
convention needs a matching certificate

00:02:28.693 --> 00:02:29.753
of its own.

00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:35.303
The ATLAS and C M S rows are separate
published Higgs-mass references.

00:02:35.303 --> 00:02:39.428
Compare the certified curvature mass with
each stated channel and error band.

00:02:39.428 --> 00:02:42.613
The listed difference is not an exclusion
significance.

00:02:42.613 --> 00:02:45.883
Connecting a one-loop curvature mass to a
measured pole mass still requires

00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.303
matching and higher-order uncertainties.

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:53.062
The dataset links explain the route to a
fuller comparison.

00:02:53.062 --> 00:02:55.887
Channels have not been combined into a
likelihood.

00:02:55.887 --> 00:03:00.132
C E R N hosts the experiments and is not a
third independent measurement.

00:03:00.132 --> 00:03:04.487
Archive the inputs and sources beside the
result; a certified numerical calculation

00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:07.192
alone does not establish agreement with
nature.

00:03:10.335 --> 00:03:15.060
Open Design a scale. The map run backwards:
name a compactification scale

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.590
and a Higgs-mass window, and get a bulk
content that lands there — or a named

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.580
certificate that none exists.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:25.478
Enter a target compactification scale of
two teraelectronvolts and run the

00:03:25.478 --> 00:03:29.773
design. Inspect the returned content or the
named certificate.

00:03:29.773 --> 00:03:34.333
A search budget is a stopping condition,
not evidence that no solution exists.

00:03:35.668 --> 00:03:39.583
budget means the search stopped, not that
the rung is empty; the page keeps

00:03:39.583 --> 00:03:43.793
the two apart on purpose. The reachable-set
panel resolves each cluster into

00:03:43.793 --> 00:03:46.213
the finite set of points it really is.

00:03:49.335 --> 00:03:54.590
Open Count a rung. How many bulk contents
sit on a rung — counted, not built.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:59.115
The counting graph is generated when the
section opens.

00:03:59.115 --> 00:04:02.160
Click a different horizontal position to
move the moment probe and compare

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.050
counts across the rungs. These are counts
of contents, not a list of experimentally

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.465
viable models.

00:04:09.802 --> 00:04:13.552
The four published-seed totals reproduce an
independent enumeration of 69

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:18.172
022 464 contents. The candidate uses even

00:04:18.172 --> 00:04:22.442
rungs and half-integral A₄; its counts are
computed separately, without transferring

00:04:22.442 --> 00:04:24.452
the published measured-mass fibre.

00:04:27.602 --> 00:04:32.207
Open Atlas. Every bulk content of at most
five multiplets — 1

00:04:32.207 --> 00:04:35.207
286 of them — with its potential drawn as
one tile.

00:04:36.535 --> 00:04:40.365
Draw the lattice of one thousand two
hundred and eighty-six contents.

00:04:40.365 --> 00:04:42.695
Each tile is a different potential.

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:47.390
Inspect its colour and shape; a flat or
blank potential is itself a result.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.235
Clicking a tile transfers that content to
the model.

00:04:51.535 --> 00:04:56.020
One tile is green in the Higgs window, and
it is the published row (2).

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.715
A blank tile is a content whose potential
is flat, and the harness predicts

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:02.080
which tiles blank before they are drawn.

00:05:05.202 --> 00:05:10.007
Open Same potential?. Whether two bulk
contents are the same one-loop potential,

00:05:10.007 --> 00:05:12.127
by Part VII's Theorem 3.

00:05:13.468 --> 00:05:17.248
Copy the first content and construct its
canonical representative.

00:05:17.248 --> 00:05:20.388
Compare the potential curves and the five
coordinates.

00:05:20.388 --> 00:05:23.618
Different lists of matter can produce
exactly the same one-loop potential

00:05:23.618 --> 00:05:25.273
under the stated theorem.

00:05:26.602 --> 00:05:29.987
Same five coordinates ⟺ identically the
same potential.

00:05:29.987 --> 00:05:34.772
The dashed curve riding exactly on the
solid one is the theorem, not a
coincidence.

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:39.568
Load the equal-moment example and inspect
the local range.

00:05:39.568 --> 00:05:43.418
These two contents have exactly the same D,
A four and G through their integer

00:05:43.418 --> 00:05:47.188
coordinates, while their endpoint
coordinate differs.

00:05:47.188 --> 00:05:50.148
Matching the small-angle expansion is a
weaker condition than matching the

00:05:50.148 --> 00:05:51.663
complete potential.

00:05:53.002 --> 00:05:55.232
Switch to the full phase interval.

00:05:55.232 --> 00:05:59.182
The plotted full potentials separate even
though the local expansions match.

00:05:59.182 --> 00:06:03.147
These curves illustrate finite Fourier
sums; the exact coordinate statement

00:06:03.147 --> 00:06:05.822
and the interval proof are recorded
separately.

00:06:05.822 --> 00:06:08.797
Use the full potential when judging a
global vacuum.

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:14.245
For content B, the endpoint at alpha equal
to one has certified energy below

00:06:14.245 --> 00:06:18.490
its local minimum. This rules out that
local minimum as global.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.625
It does not prove that the endpoint itself
is global.

00:06:21.625 --> 00:06:25.225
Save the comparison to retain the exact
inputs and the scope of this mathematical

00:06:25.225 --> 00:06:26.290
example.

00:06:29.402 --> 00:06:33.992
Open Anomalies & proton. What each
multiplet contributes to the anomaly bill,

00:06:33.992 --> 00:06:37.167
in eighths, and what the proton-decay
escape costs.

00:06:38.468 --> 00:06:42.163
Read the signed contributions to the
anomaly bill and the rung ladder.

00:06:42.163 --> 00:06:46.753
A nonzero bulk bill does not, by itself,
make the theory inconsistent:

00:06:46.753 --> 00:06:49.768
possible brane matter must be considered
separately.

00:06:49.768 --> 00:06:53.378
The research card later in this section
studies a different warped model.

00:06:54.668 --> 00:06:58.603
A non-zero bill is not an inconsistency:
brane fermions pay into the same

00:06:58.603 --> 00:07:00.723
channels with the opposite sign.

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:03.638
What the ladder forbids is a rung, not a
model.

00:07:06.735 --> 00:07:09.030
Open Escape from proton decay.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:13.200
The escape from proton decay, constructed:
type a brane content and watch

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.050
the six channels, the assignments and the
selection rule recompute.

00:07:18.335 --> 00:07:22.225
Choose the scalar charge associated with
the eighty-four representation.

00:07:22.225 --> 00:07:26.020
The rational anomaly channels and charge
assignments update together.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.430
Inspect which assignments meet the
displayed proton-protection rule; the

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.830
calculation does not supply a measured
proton lifetime.

00:07:34.135 --> 00:07:37.635
Every assignment that protects the proton
can also cancel all six channels —

00:07:37.635 --> 00:07:40.070
protection never costs an anomaly.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.790
That is an enumeration, not an argument.

00:07:45.935 --> 00:07:48.290
Open Multiplets & parities.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:52.235
The layer under the term tables: every
representation broken into multiplets

00:07:52.235 --> 00:07:54.030
with their three Z₂ parities.

00:07:55.335 --> 00:07:59.275
Select the eighty-four representation and
change its parity assignment.

00:07:59.275 --> 00:08:03.505
The decomposition and parity picture update
from the representation data.

00:08:03.505 --> 00:08:06.800
Use the table to connect a surviving zero
mode with the sign of its potential

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.125
contribution.

00:08:09.468 --> 00:08:12.558
The term tables here are DERIVED and then
checked against the ones the rest

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.483
of the page computes with.

00:08:14.483 --> 00:08:18.013
If the two ever disagreed, this panel is
where it would show.

00:08:21.135 --> 00:08:25.660
Open Screen a table. Arithmetic screens for
a published row, with conditional

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:29.115
rung certificates and independent full-
potential witness checks.

00:08:30.402 --> 00:08:34.277
Enter a candidate resonance at four
thousand gigaelectronvolts.

00:08:34.277 --> 00:08:37.502
The comb samples nearby teeth for the
displayed rungs.

00:08:37.502 --> 00:08:41.442
Its filters, spacing table and download now
use the same conditional-bound

00:08:41.442 --> 00:08:43.557
check for the chosen inputs.

00:08:43.557 --> 00:08:47.102
A surviving tooth is not proof of an
attainable physical vacuum.

00:08:48.402 --> 00:08:52.412
The moment ceilings remain conditional on
the small-angle approximation.

00:08:52.412 --> 00:08:55.767
The five printed phase rounding intervals
contain no stationary point for

00:08:55.767 --> 00:08:57.737
the implemented potential.

00:08:57.737 --> 00:09:02.157
All eight fermion coefficient tables now
agree with an independent reconstruction.

00:09:02.157 --> 00:09:05.487
The physical gauge determinant and the
origin of the printed numerical mismatch

00:09:05.487 --> 00:09:06.697
remain open.

00:09:08.002 --> 00:09:11.057
Expand Certified S U seven benchmarks.

00:09:11.057 --> 00:09:15.202
Twenty-six Lean theorems verify finite
algebraic identities.

00:09:15.202 --> 00:09:18.602
Separate interval proofs certify ten global
minima on the stated one-phase

00:09:18.602 --> 00:09:23.017
domain: five reference contents under each
of two gauge prescriptions.

00:09:23.017 --> 00:09:26.372
These statements do not certify an
arbitrary edited model.

00:09:27.668 --> 00:09:29.873
Download the certificate bundle.

00:09:29.873 --> 00:09:34.578
It contains rational enclosures, continuum
partitions, checksums and reproduction

00:09:34.578 --> 00:09:38.793
records. The infinite Fourier analysis is
computer-assisted and has not been

00:09:38.793 --> 00:09:43.193
formalized in Lean. A global-minimum
certificate is conditional on its potential

00:09:43.193 --> 00:09:46.918
and assumptions; it is not a certificate of
physical viability.

00:09:48.268 --> 00:09:52.633
The English dossier assigns the model to
Komori and Maru, the expansion antecedents

00:09:52.633 --> 00:09:57.233
to Haba, Takenaga and Yamashita and to
Sakamoto and Takenaga, and the S U

00:09:57.233 --> 00:10:00.448
seven application in Part Seven to Carles
Marín.

00:10:00.448 --> 00:10:03.518
Verification does not establish
originality.

00:10:03.518 --> 00:10:06.908
The dossier keeps the remaining physical
questions explicit.

00:10:10.002 --> 00:10:14.562
Open Collider. Which state a dijet search
bounds, plus a separate Higgs-rate

00:10:14.562 --> 00:10:17.702
scenario checked against pinned
experimental datasets.

00:10:19.002 --> 00:10:21.952
Move the dijet mass probe to five point
two.

00:10:21.952 --> 00:10:25.612
Read the selected state, mass scale and
published comparison under its colour

00:10:25.612 --> 00:10:30.177
hypothesis. The separate Higgs experiment
below uses another explicitly supplied

00:10:30.177 --> 00:10:31.662
scalar scenario.

00:10:33.002 --> 00:10:36.567
The dijet delta chi  squared  values are
quoted from the published record;

00:10:36.567 --> 00:10:39.467
the Higgs experiment computes its own
comparison.

00:10:39.467 --> 00:10:44.287
Its 159 HiggsSignals observables are not
159 independent degrees

00:10:44.287 --> 00:10:48.712
of freedom. Coupling and width assumptions
can compensate in visible rates,

00:10:48.712 --> 00:10:52.202
so this scalar comparison is not a complete
G H U fit.

00:10:53.535 --> 00:10:57.715
The displayed recast table belongs to the
earlier archived calculation.

00:10:57.715 --> 00:11:02.110
Separately, the dossier now records the C M
S angular dataset at one hundred

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:07.080
thirty-eight inverse femtobarns: seven
distributions and seventy-seven bins.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.080
Integrity checks validate the imported
tables.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.130
Importing them has not produced a new G H U
fit.

00:11:14.468 --> 00:11:18.308
The released correlation matrix describes
fitted signal strengths.

00:11:18.308 --> 00:11:21.738
It cannot automatically be used as the
covariance of the normalized unfolded

00:11:21.738 --> 00:11:26.923
distributions. A new reinterpretation
requires the G H U signal, selections

00:11:26.923 --> 00:11:30.833
and nuisance parameters, with a justified
statistical model.

00:11:30.833 --> 00:11:35.283
The dossier distinguishes reviewed
resources from analyses actually executed.

00:11:38.402 --> 00:11:42.797
Open Selection rule. Which alpha -domain a
search may legally use, and which

00:11:42.797 --> 00:11:46.222
S U 4 representations can hold a quark
generation.

00:11:47.535 --> 00:11:52.030
Select the representation with Dynkin
labels one, zero, one.

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:55.255
The centre-charge and chiral-projection
results determine which search domain

00:11:55.255 --> 00:11:58.260
is permitted and which content can host a
generation.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:02.580
The domain is computed from the
representation, not chosen for convenience.

00:12:03.868 --> 00:12:07.328
The centre-charge gate is classical and is
credited as such.

00:12:07.328 --> 00:12:10.708
What is ours is the chiral projection that
gives the closed count.

00:12:13.802 --> 00:12:18.457
Open Model calculator. A matter content in,
the Higgs out — the AHMN

00:12:18.457 --> 00:12:20.657
model recomputed in front of you.

00:12:22.002 --> 00:12:24.742
Load the named A H M N reference model.

00:12:24.742 --> 00:12:29.592
Its terms, potential and derived quantities
are recalculated by the browser.

00:12:29.592 --> 00:12:33.012
Start with this anchored content before
changing multiplicities or boundary

00:12:33.012 --> 00:12:37.607
signs, and retain the stated normalization
caveat for absolute scales.

00:12:38.935 --> 00:12:42.535
The mass ratio carries no normalisation and
no caveat.

00:12:42.535 --> 00:12:44.185
Absolute scales do.

00:12:47.335 --> 00:12:51.615
Open eta-meter. What the boundary sign eta
does to a multiplet's contribution —

00:12:51.615 --> 00:12:54.265
in closed form, from one integer.

00:12:55.602 --> 00:12:59.287
Load the supplied example made from a blind
multiplet, then flip every eta

00:12:59.287 --> 00:13:03.907
sign. In this example the potential is
insensitive to that change.

00:13:03.907 --> 00:13:07.447
The eta-difference view exposes the
cancellation; it is not evidence that

00:13:07.447 --> 00:13:09.407
boundary signs never matter.

00:13:10.735 --> 00:13:13.945
In difference mode every blind multiplet
goes blank.

00:13:13.945 --> 00:13:17.020
That is Part V's theorem seen without
reading a number.

00:13:20.135 --> 00:13:24.905
Open Five dimensions. Haba–Yamashita's own
5D S U 3 model,

00:13:24.905 --> 00:13:27.300
with the vacuum their paper leaves undone.

00:13:28.602 --> 00:13:31.917
Compare the pure-gauge case with the
supplied marginal trio.

00:13:31.917 --> 00:13:34.382
Read the potential and vacuum together.

00:13:34.382 --> 00:13:38.287
This panel uses the five-dimensional S U
three reference, so its arithmetic

00:13:38.287 --> 00:13:42.532
and assumptions must not be borrowed from
the six-dimensional S U seven benchmark.

00:13:43.868 --> 00:13:47.558
Pure gauge never breaks the symmetry (D =
−9).

00:13:47.558 --> 00:13:51.953
In this whole 5D class 8D is even: the odd
rung the S U 7 ceiling stands

00:13:51.953 --> 00:13:53.898
on needs the sixth dimension.

00:13:57.002 --> 00:14:01.582
Open the S U N builder. Boundary conditions
and supported bulk representations

00:14:01.582 --> 00:14:05.857
define the implemented one-loop Wilson-line
potential in five dimensions.

00:14:05.857 --> 00:14:07.527
The model is the input.

00:14:08.868 --> 00:14:11.818
Load section four point three of the source
paper.

00:14:11.818 --> 00:14:15.053
This pure-gauge example prefers the
endpoint at one.

00:14:15.053 --> 00:14:18.733
Read the surviving group from the joint
commutant: being at an endpoint does

00:14:18.733 --> 00:14:21.608
not prove that the generators at zero
survive.

00:14:21.608 --> 00:14:26.048
The potential plot and the reported minimum
now use the same V over C normalization.

00:14:27.335 --> 00:14:31.155
The four worked examples are checked
against the implemented formulas.

00:14:31.155 --> 00:14:35.765
With one Wilson phase, the cosine terms
admit the same moment construction.

00:14:35.765 --> 00:14:39.520
Its closed stationary branch is a small-
angle approximation.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:43.150
The numerical Fourier minimum is a separate
calculation, and a finite grid

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:45.530
is not a certificate of the global minimum.

00:14:48.668 --> 00:14:53.198
Open Paper models. Four models somebody
else published, taken off their

00:14:53.198 --> 00:14:55.768
pages and run through this instrument's
engine.

00:14:57.068 --> 00:15:01.208
Change the triplet Dirac-fermion count to
five in the supported reference.

00:15:01.208 --> 00:15:05.728
Inspect which outputs are actually computed
and which remain structural statements.

00:15:05.728 --> 00:15:09.388
Where offered, the load button transfers
the supported content into the general

00:15:09.388 --> 00:15:10.333
builder.

00:15:11.668 --> 00:15:15.053
Three of the four are supersymmetric and
this engine's potential is not,

00:15:15.053 --> 00:15:18.813
so their rows are boundary-condition and
zero-mode statements only.

00:15:18.813 --> 00:15:22.918
An amber row disagrees with one printed
equation, not with a paper, and names

00:15:22.918 --> 00:15:25.733
which of that paper's own equations agrees
with which.

00:15:28.868 --> 00:15:33.513
Open 4D spectrum. What the model on the
builder CONTAINS: the four-dimensional

00:15:33.513 --> 00:15:35.878
fields and their Kaluza–Klein towers.

00:15:37.202 --> 00:15:40.742
Load the S U six example and move the
Wilson-line probe.

00:15:40.742 --> 00:15:44.132
Compare the potential families with the
exact low-lying tower.

00:15:44.132 --> 00:15:47.022
The lowest levels can require a different
reading from the families used

00:15:47.022 --> 00:15:48.767
to construct the potential.

00:15:50.068 --> 00:15:54.168
The cosine families reconstruct the
potential, but do not by themselves
determine

00:15:54.168 --> 00:15:56.708
every lowest level of the exact tower.

00:15:56.708 --> 00:16:00.868
The moved probe is a chosen phase, not
necessarily a vacuum.

00:16:00.868 --> 00:16:04.518
Read its surviving group separately; a
boundary coordinate alone does not

00:16:04.518 --> 00:16:06.383
determine symmetry breaking.

00:16:09.468 --> 00:16:14.043
Open Anomalies. What that content owes:
every anomaly channel of the unbroken

00:16:14.043 --> 00:16:16.358
group, in exact rationals.

00:16:17.668 --> 00:16:22.013
Load the S U six example. The ledger
evaluates each anomaly channel of the

00:16:22.013 --> 00:16:25.013
unbroken group in exact rational
arithmetic.

00:16:25.013 --> 00:16:29.323
Distinguish a vanishing bill from a case
with no massless fermions to test.

00:16:29.323 --> 00:16:32.078
Brane cancellation is a separate
calculation.

00:16:33.402 --> 00:16:37.632
A non-zero row is a BILL, not an
inconsistency: the brane fermions such a

00:16:37.632 --> 00:16:41.112
model needs pay into the same channels with
the opposite sign.

00:16:41.112 --> 00:16:44.162
And a model with no massless fermion has
nothing to cancel — that is no

00:16:44.162 --> 00:16:46.382
subject, not cancels.

00:16:49.468 --> 00:16:54.038
Open Brane matter. Who pays that bill, and
what it costs: matter on the

00:16:54.038 --> 00:16:58.238
two fixed points, held at once to the
anomaly ledger and to Part I's boundary-
mass

00:16:58.238 --> 00:16:59.133
gate.

00:17:00.468 --> 00:17:04.193
Load the supplied S U five brane example
and solve its supported linear charge

00:17:04.193 --> 00:17:08.908
channels. Compare the anomaly ledger with
the surviving massless fields.

00:17:08.908 --> 00:17:12.568
Paying an anomaly bill and giving a field a
mass are different requirements.

00:17:13.868 --> 00:17:18.453
Two verdicts, kept apart on purpose: the
bill before and after, and the massless

00:17:18.453 --> 00:17:22.998
count before and after. The same field can
pay one and not the other, because

00:17:22.998 --> 00:17:26.288
the charge that cancels an anomaly is not
in general the charge a mass term

00:17:26.288 --> 00:17:30.418
needs. The surviving count is a lower bound
— the rank test assumes generic

00:17:30.418 --> 00:17:31.593
couplings.

00:17:34.735 --> 00:17:39.005
Open Scan. Walk the displayed finite space
of boundary conditions and matter

00:17:39.005 --> 00:17:41.480
contents through the selected filters.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:45.110
The breaking filter tests which generators
at phase zero survive at the numerically

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.475
located minimum.

00:17:47.802 --> 00:17:50.042
Run the displayed finite sweep.

00:17:50.042 --> 00:17:54.012
Read the denominator, each filter in the
funnel, the survivors and the unresolved

00:17:54.012 --> 00:17:59.027
cases. The interface counts boundary
conditions and equivalence classes
separately,

00:17:59.027 --> 00:18:02.372
so surviving rows are not automatically
distinct theories.

00:18:03.668 --> 00:18:07.638
Surviving boundary conditions are not
surviving theories: [p,q,r,s] ~ [p−

00:18:07.638 --> 00:18:13.318
1,q+1,r+1,s−1] is the same theory, so the
headline is a pair of numbers.

00:18:13.318 --> 00:18:16.238
An undecided vacuum is counted apart from a
no.

00:18:19.335 --> 00:18:21.900
Open One model, every verdict.

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.055
Which of the instrument's answers about
this model are about the THEORY,

00:18:25.055 --> 00:18:27.730
and which only about the frame you are
standing in.

00:18:29.068 --> 00:18:32.518
Start the built-in example, then choose its
displayed equivalent boundary

00:18:32.518 --> 00:18:36.863
condition. Compare the tags identifying
properties of the theory and properties

00:18:36.863 --> 00:18:40.953
of the chosen frame. A changed coordinate
description can move a displayed

00:18:40.953 --> 00:18:43.493
quantity without changing the physical
theory.

00:18:44.802 --> 00:18:48.132
The tags compare quantities in the
evaluated frames.

00:18:48.132 --> 00:18:51.762
Read boundary coordinates and the surviving
symmetry separately.

00:18:51.762 --> 00:18:54.892
Reported energies share the potential
normalization.

00:18:54.892 --> 00:18:58.912
Numerical agreement between the located
minima is evidence for these evaluations,

00:18:58.912 --> 00:19:01.492
not an exhaustive global-minimum
certificate.

00:19:04.602 --> 00:19:09.297
Open Simulator. Choose builder predictions,
a top-Kaluza Klein Higgs-rate

00:19:09.297 --> 00:19:13.302
reference or the neutrino ring; compare
calculated responses with their explicitly

00:19:13.302 --> 00:19:15.532
scoped experimental references.

00:19:16.868 --> 00:19:20.518
Load the published builder example and set
the gauge coupling to one.

00:19:20.518 --> 00:19:24.463
Higgs masses now use analytic Fourier
second derivatives, with an explicit

00:19:24.463 --> 00:19:29.133
truncation error bound. The export retains
the actual coupling and phase,

00:19:29.133 --> 00:19:32.718
and distinguishes a chosen probe from a
numerically located minimum.

00:19:34.068 --> 00:19:37.343
The thermal history belongs to a separate S
U three benchmark and the stated

00:19:37.343 --> 00:19:41.323
supported regime. It is not a detector
forecast or the thermal history of

00:19:41.323 --> 00:19:45.763
this builder. At commuting endpoints, the
present mass dictionary does not

00:19:45.763 --> 00:19:49.153
identify a shifted W candidate to set the
scale.

00:19:49.153 --> 00:19:53.218
That limitation does not imply that the
phase-zero gauge group survives.

00:19:56.335 --> 00:20:00.675
Open Boundary conditions. Which boundary
conditions are the same theory,

00:20:00.675 --> 00:20:03.395
walked as orbits rather than quoted as a
theorem.

00:20:04.735 --> 00:20:08.680
Switch from the interval orbifold to the
two-dimensional Z three example.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:13.020
The orbit structure and class count change
with the specified boundary problem.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:17.555
Equivalence classes identify descriptions
connected by the allowed transformations.

00:20:18.868 --> 00:20:23.548
The counts come out (N+1) squared  at every
N on S¹/

00:20:23.548 --> 00:20:27.743
Z₂, which is Haba–Hosotani–Kawamura's
theorem as a measurement.

00:20:27.743 --> 00:20:30.788
Press T squared /Z₃ and the answer changes.

00:20:33.935 --> 00:20:36.195
Open Classify an orbifold.

00:20:36.195 --> 00:20:39.910
The alphabet of an orbifold, derived from
its rotation matrix and nothing

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:40.865
else.

00:20:42.202 --> 00:20:45.872
Select the two-dimensional Z four rotation
and classify it.

00:20:45.872 --> 00:20:49.187
The group-theoretic alphabet is derived
from the matrix.

00:20:49.187 --> 00:20:53.242
You can enter another supported matrix, but
an invalid finite-order hypothesis

00:20:53.242 --> 00:20:56.127
must be refused rather than silently
classified.

00:20:57.468 --> 00:20:59.768
Nothing is entered but the matrix.

00:20:59.768 --> 00:21:03.663
A matrix of infinite order, or one whose
characteristic polynomial is not

00:21:03.663 --> 00:21:08.538
a power of the m-th cyclotomic, comes back
REFUSED rather than classified.

00:21:11.668 --> 00:21:15.743
Open Name the relations. Which equivalence
relation on boundary conditions

00:21:15.743 --> 00:21:20.263
the literature already owns, and whether a
proposed move set connects a class.

00:21:21.602 --> 00:21:25.872
Switch to the Z four boundary problem and
judge the displayed proposed move.

00:21:25.872 --> 00:21:29.567
Read the local and global qualifications
and the source attribution.

00:21:29.567 --> 00:21:33.327
A useful move in one boundary problem is
not automatically a complete equivalence

00:21:33.327 --> 00:21:34.772
relation in another.

00:21:36.068 --> 00:21:39.808
The local/global distinction is the one
that gets misquoted, and the page

00:21:39.808 --> 00:21:41.453
carries it explicitly.

00:21:44.602 --> 00:21:47.132
Open Conjugate boundary conditions.

00:21:47.132 --> 00:21:51.522
How many inequivalent CONJUGATE boundary
conditions there are on S¹/Z₂ —

00:21:51.522 --> 00:21:55.702
and, because that count rests on a
hypothesis nobody has settled, what each

00:21:55.702 --> 00:21:57.417
of the two answers would be.

00:21:58.735 --> 00:22:02.900
Increase the group rank and compare the two
hypotheses printed in the table.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:05.845
Conjugate twists transform by congruence.

00:22:05.845 --> 00:22:09.240
Independent boundary transformations and
one linked transformation lead to

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.885
different classification questions, so the
hypothesis travels with the count.

00:22:15.202 --> 00:22:18.782
What the panel puts on screen is the
disagreement, not a number, and that

00:22:18.782 --> 00:22:23.157
is the point: the twists move by congruence
rather than similarity, and independence

00:22:23.157 --> 00:22:27.187
decides between four classes flat in N and
no finite count at all.

00:22:27.187 --> 00:22:30.162
What the label leaves unbroken is not a
property of the label — the same

00:22:30.162 --> 00:22:33.127
verdict the ordinary panel reaches by
another route.

00:22:33.127 --> 00:22:37.012
The exported card carries the hypothesis
beside the count, so a number never

00:22:37.012 --> 00:22:39.472
travels without the condition it rests on.

00:22:42.602 --> 00:22:46.737
Open Brane kinetic terms. What happens to
the tower when brane-localised

00:22:46.737 --> 00:22:50.022
kinetic terms make the masses stop being
n/R.

00:22:51.335 --> 00:22:54.310
Increase the brane kinetic coefficient to
twelve.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:58.600
The spectrum responds because the roots are
no longer a simple integer tower.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:02.740
Compare the root conditions and
normalization, then use the separate
warped-unification

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.960
card for coupling-running questions.

00:23:06.268 --> 00:23:09.493
The special functions are checked against
mpmath at forty digits, and the

00:23:09.493 --> 00:23:13.088
c  to  0 limit is computed in closed form
by code that shares nothing with

00:23:13.088 --> 00:23:16.658
the solver. That limit found three real
defects.

00:23:19.802 --> 00:23:24.367
Open Gravity–gauge · 3D. Which physical
information a complete tower of masses

00:23:24.367 --> 00:23:27.497
leaves undetermined in a controlled
gravity-gauge example.

00:23:28.802 --> 00:23:32.767
Select eta equal to minus zero point nine,
then display the gauge kinetic

00:23:32.767 --> 00:23:37.722
function. The paired masses and the source
response carry different information.

00:23:37.722 --> 00:23:42.452
The Wilson quantity here is a kinetic
normalization, not a Higgs-mass prediction.

00:23:43.802 --> 00:23:47.187
The positive paired tensor NN and vector DD
masses are protected by their

00:23:47.187 --> 00:23:49.277
common canonical operator.

00:23:49.277 --> 00:23:52.687
The gauge NN control has different
boundaries and moves.

00:23:52.687 --> 00:23:56.762
The source position is in the extra
dimension, not a laboratory location;

00:23:56.762 --> 00:23:59.732
the Wilson kinetic scale is not a Higgs
mass.

00:23:59.732 --> 00:24:04.302
For p=1.1 a canonical bulk scalar realizes
the family, with stability

00:24:04.302 --> 00:24:05.962
and cutoff still open.

00:24:09.068 --> 00:24:13.273
Open The literature. The reading list
behind the series: what each paper

00:24:13.273 --> 00:24:16.663
publishes, and which ones a person has
actually read.

00:24:18.002 --> 00:24:21.132
Filter the reading list to the complete-
triple selection.

00:24:21.132 --> 00:24:25.042
Inspect the recorded sources and what each
paper actually supplies.

00:24:25.042 --> 00:24:29.022
Keyword signals help navigate this corpus;
they are not proofs, and this

00:24:29.022 --> 00:24:31.817
reading list is not an exhaustive map of
the field.

00:24:33.135 --> 00:24:38.005
A signal is a keyword, and a keyword sweep
misses whatever is phrased differently.

00:24:38.005 --> 00:24:41.475
Nothing is asserted from a signal alone,
and the corpus is our reading list

00:24:41.475 --> 00:24:42.865
rather than the field.

00:24:46.002 --> 00:24:50.427
The second Simulator mode is the top Kaluza
Klein Higgs-production reference.

00:24:50.427 --> 00:24:54.332
Its leading rate, finite tower and resummed
comparison answer one specified

00:24:54.332 --> 00:24:55.697
model question.

00:24:57.002 --> 00:25:01.157
Move the Kaluza Klein scale to fifteen
hundred gigaelectronvolts.

00:25:01.157 --> 00:25:04.372
Inspect the rate curve and the separate
convergence display.

00:25:04.372 --> 00:25:08.452
The chosen comparison window is
conditional, and a custom window is a user-
supplied

00:25:08.452 --> 00:25:09.562
scenario.

00:25:12.668 --> 00:25:15.293
Select the four-dimensional neutrino ring.

00:25:15.293 --> 00:25:18.618
Calibration can hold the light mass and
active-current deficit fixed while

00:25:18.618 --> 00:25:21.193
the required couplings are reconstructed.

00:25:21.193 --> 00:25:23.768
Six heavy pairs belong to this base model.

00:25:25.068 --> 00:25:29.333
Compare mu B equal to zero with five
thousand kilo electron volts.

00:25:29.333 --> 00:25:33.328
The leading light mass and the heavy pair
splittings carry different information.

00:25:33.328 --> 00:25:36.663
Read the approximation indicators alongside
the spectrum.

00:25:39.802 --> 00:25:43.212
The C M S comparison uses published
observed and expected curves with their

00:25:43.212 --> 00:25:47.972
bands. The overlay keeps the selected
flavour and Dirac or Majorana hypothesis

00:25:47.972 --> 00:25:48.972
visible.

00:25:50.268 --> 00:25:53.308
Choose muon flavour and the Majorana
reference.

00:25:53.308 --> 00:25:57.713
Inspect the per-component convention, mass
domain and reference ratios.

00:25:57.713 --> 00:26:01.408
The ring overlay is not a likelihood that
includes its complete flavour structure,

00:26:01.408 --> 00:26:04.388
decay acceptance and multi-state
interference.

00:26:07.535 --> 00:26:10.590
The decay card starts from the weak-channel
scenario.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.110
Select a heavy pair and inspect where its
width goes, its lifetime and the

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:16.015
fixed-boost flight estimate.

00:26:17.335 --> 00:26:20.110
Include the calculated Majoron channels.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.090
The width and the derived lifetime respond
together.

00:26:23.090 --> 00:26:26.135
An extra width is a separate user
hypothesis.

00:26:26.135 --> 00:26:30.035
Read the isolated-pair and overlap
diagnostics before interpreting the same-
sign

00:26:30.035 --> 00:26:31.910
over opposite-sign ratio.

00:26:33.202 --> 00:26:36.482
Change the fixed boost to ten and inspect
the flight curve.

00:26:36.482 --> 00:26:39.962
This illustrates the supplied kinematic
scenario; it does not integrate a

00:26:39.962 --> 00:26:42.707
detector acceptance or a production
spectrum.

00:26:45.802 --> 00:26:50.432
Three sterile copies reconstruct the chosen
light masses and mixing orientation.

00:26:50.432 --> 00:26:54.352
The mass ordering, phases and active
deficits are inputs.

00:26:54.352 --> 00:26:57.597
The card also displays eighteen heavy-pair
flavour weights.

00:26:58.935 --> 00:27:01.745
Change the Dirac phase to ninety degrees.

00:27:01.745 --> 00:27:05.455
Compare neutrino and antineutrino
appearance in the unitary vacuum limit,

00:27:05.455 --> 00:27:07.795
then inspect the flavour fractions.

00:27:07.795 --> 00:27:11.480
Majorana phases cancel from these
oscillation curves but can affect the
displayed

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:13.570
light-sector double-beta quantity.

00:27:16.668 --> 00:27:20.233
This experiment holds the chosen light
masses and mixing orientation fixed

00:27:20.233 --> 00:27:22.803
while reconstructing a parameter path.

00:27:22.803 --> 00:27:25.293
The green strip tells you what is fixed.

00:27:25.293 --> 00:27:29.793
The five figures separate heavy masses,
splitting, current factors, small

00:27:29.793 --> 00:27:31.788
differences and reference data.

00:27:33.135 --> 00:27:36.300
Move the selected position from one tenth
to eight tenths along the mu B

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:40.585
path. The mass stays fixed while the pair
splitting changes.

00:27:40.585 --> 00:27:43.325
The open circle tracks the selected point.

00:27:43.325 --> 00:27:47.390
Equal light inputs therefore do not
determine every heavy-sector response.

00:27:48.735 --> 00:27:52.165
Now choose the common active deficit and
its upper endpoint.

00:27:52.165 --> 00:27:56.335
The raw vacuum current factor is
suppressed, but the specified near
normalization

00:27:56.335 --> 00:27:58.395
cancels the common factor.

00:27:58.395 --> 00:28:01.625
The difference figure labels numerical
coincidence rather than amplifying

00:28:01.625 --> 00:28:02.720
roundoff.

00:28:04.068 --> 00:28:06.298
Choose deficit direction three.

00:28:06.298 --> 00:28:09.213
Unequal deficits can leave a shape
response.

00:28:09.213 --> 00:28:12.373
The fourth graph magnifies an absolute
difference that is difficult to see

00:28:12.373 --> 00:28:14.363
in the overlapping curves.

00:28:14.363 --> 00:28:18.863
Its scale is explicit, and the exported
samples retain their original precision.

00:28:20.202 --> 00:28:23.607
The DeepCore map uses the archived twenty-
eighteen standard-three-neutrino

00:28:23.607 --> 00:28:28.032
tables. The cross stays fixed along the
ring path because these light inputs

00:28:28.032 --> 00:28:32.147
stay fixed. The map is not a nonunitary
ring likelihood and is not added

00:28:32.147 --> 00:28:33.532
to NuFIT.

00:28:34.868 --> 00:28:38.363
To move the cross, change sine squared
theta twenty-three in the flavour

00:28:38.363 --> 00:28:41.923
card. Here it is set to zero point six.

00:28:41.923 --> 00:28:44.578
The reference updates for the new light
input.

00:28:44.578 --> 00:28:48.953
Outside its published grid the laboratory
withholds a value instead of extrapolating.

00:28:52.068 --> 00:28:56.603
This paper-model experiment reconstructs
the stated flat S U six potential.

00:28:56.603 --> 00:29:00.228
Matter content and the winding cutoff are
explicit controls.

00:29:00.228 --> 00:29:03.468
A small Wilson minimum must be read with
its convergence check.

00:29:04.802 --> 00:29:08.897
Set the winding cutoff to ten and inspect
the shifted minimum and comparison.

00:29:08.897 --> 00:29:12.157
A finite truncation can change the apparent
result.

00:29:12.157 --> 00:29:15.517
The supported potential can be transferred
to the builder; this does not

00:29:15.517 --> 00:29:17.762
certify a complete flavour model.

00:29:20.868 --> 00:29:23.913
The warped-unification card compares
differential gauge running under the

00:29:23.913 --> 00:29:25.933
selected matter assignment.

00:29:25.933 --> 00:29:29.373
Required ultraviolet boundary terms and
their residuals are outputs of that

00:29:29.373 --> 00:29:31.073
stated approximation.

00:29:32.402 --> 00:29:34.937
Switch the matter assignment to C two.

00:29:34.937 --> 00:29:38.487
The running differences and required
boundary terms respond.

00:29:38.487 --> 00:29:41.837
The naive-dimensional-analysis reference is
a scale estimate.

00:29:41.837 --> 00:29:46.152
The separate localization probe is not a
complete fermion-spectrum calculation.

00:29:49.268 --> 00:29:52.213
The thermal card is its own S U three
benchmark.

00:29:52.213 --> 00:29:56.023
It uses named paper cases and compares
potential cutoffs.

00:29:56.023 --> 00:29:59.408
A stored matching PhaseTracer result is
available offline for the recorded

00:29:59.408 --> 00:30:00.508
inputs.

00:30:01.802 --> 00:30:05.332
Load paper case two and compare the
potential and phase flow.

00:30:05.332 --> 00:30:08.997
Coexistence and a nucleation proxy are
different questions.

00:30:08.997 --> 00:30:12.632
A new point needs a matching bounce
calculation; changing the builder model

00:30:12.632 --> 00:30:15.642
does not turn this benchmark into its
thermal history.

00:30:16.935 --> 00:30:20.625
The new comparison runs PhaseTracer and
CosmoTransitions on the same S U

00:30:20.625 --> 00:30:23.020
three potential and canonical field.

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:26.055
Nine matched temperatures compare the
bounce action.

00:30:26.055 --> 00:30:29.730
The programs share shooting-method
ancestry, so agreement is a numerical

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:34.270
cross-check, not an independent interval
proof or an experimental validation.

00:30:35.602 --> 00:30:39.372
The table changes the spatial and thermal
Fourier cutoffs together.

00:30:39.372 --> 00:30:43.312
T one-forty solves S three over T equal to
one hundred forty.

00:30:43.312 --> 00:30:48.232
It is a proxy, separate from the integrated
nucleation and percolation results.

00:30:48.232 --> 00:30:51.852
The older displayed proxy used a looser
shooting tolerance; compare like

00:30:51.852 --> 00:30:54.242
settings before interpreting a difference.

00:30:55.535 --> 00:30:57.900
Open the numerical diagnostics.

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:01.565
Tolerance shifts, profile sampling and the
virial residual test different

00:31:01.565 --> 00:31:03.700
aspects of the calculation.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:07.730
The targets are numerical criteria, not
confidence levels.

00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:12.600
Higher loops, thermal resummation and
plasma dynamics remain unquantified.

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:16.850
These S U three examples are separate from
the S U seven mass certificates.

00:31:18.135 --> 00:31:20.770
Change the coupling away from the
reference.

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:24.270
The archived solver comparison disappears
because all thermal inputs must

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:28.620
match. A stale certificate must not follow
an edited point.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:32.185
Load a named paper case again to recover
its evidence; a new point needs

00:31:32.185 --> 00:31:33.720
a new calculation.

00:31:35.068 --> 00:31:38.383
Reload case two and save the experiment
JSON.

00:31:38.383 --> 00:31:42.913
It retains both solver versions,
tolerances, cutoffs and sources.

00:31:42.913 --> 00:31:46.818
The thermal potential is credited to Hirose
and Shibuya, CosmoTransitions

00:31:46.818 --> 00:31:51.008
to Carroll Wainwright, and PhaseTracer to
Athron and collaborators.

00:31:51.008 --> 00:31:53.713
Keep this provenance with every exported
result.

00:31:56.802 --> 00:32:00.422
Integrated transition history uses refined
action tables.

00:32:00.422 --> 00:32:04.052
Read nucleation, percolation and completion
separately.

00:32:04.052 --> 00:32:07.587
The false-vacuum fraction and physical-
volume condition make those distinctions

00:32:07.587 --> 00:32:08.582
visible.

00:32:09.868 --> 00:32:13.133
Set the assumed wall speed to zero point
eight and the fluid efficiency to

00:32:13.133 --> 00:32:17.923
zero point one. Compare the history and
conditional acoustic spectrum.

00:32:17.923 --> 00:32:21.728
Wall friction, plasma evolution and
detector significance are not calculated

00:32:21.728 --> 00:32:23.258
by these controls.

00:32:26.402 --> 00:32:30.337
This warped-model experiment separates
ultraviolet and infrared anomaly factors,

00:32:30.337 --> 00:32:33.597
gauge cancellation and the baryon-current
matrix.

00:32:33.597 --> 00:32:37.637
Its sources and approximation are distinct
from the base S U seven ledger.

00:32:38.935 --> 00:32:42.425
Remove the lepton generations in this test
configuration.

00:32:42.425 --> 00:32:45.885
The imbalance exposes a gauge anomaly that
needs completion.

00:32:45.885 --> 00:32:49.860
A nonzero baryon-current anomaly is a
separate statement; it is not a computed

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:52.630
proton lifetime or baryogenesis yield.

00:32:55.735 --> 00:32:59.125
Begin with the stored Standard Model-like
scalar reference.

00:32:59.125 --> 00:33:02.725
Local couplings determine production rates,
partial widths and branching

00:33:02.725 --> 00:33:06.945
fractions. Matching HiggsBounds and
HiggsSignals calculations use pinned

00:33:06.945 --> 00:33:08.820
experimental datasets.

00:33:10.135 --> 00:33:13.175
Add one mega electron volt of invisible
width.

00:33:13.175 --> 00:33:16.835
The local predictions update, and the old
experimental result is withdrawn

00:33:16.835 --> 00:33:19.390
because its inputs no longer match.

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:22.595
Run the optional engine or import a
matching result before assigning a new

00:33:22.595 --> 00:33:24.235
experimental verdict.

00:33:27.335 --> 00:33:30.680
Open the first Kaluza Klein gluon card in
Collider and load the published

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:33.880
R S point of Casagrande and collaborators.

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:37.300
The card computes the couplings of a warped
Kaluza Klein gluon to zero-mode

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:41.270
quarks, its width and branching fractions,
and compares its leading-order

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:45.490
rates with the ATLAS top-pair limit and the
C M S dijet limit as r, prediction

00:33:45.490 --> 00:33:46.635
over limit.

00:33:47.935 --> 00:33:52.015
At three point seven five tera electron
volts, r for the top pair is close

00:33:52.015 --> 00:33:55.585
to one: the two thousand eight reference
point sits at the edge of the ATLAS

00:33:55.585 --> 00:34:00.205
comparison. That is a comparison with
ATLAS's thirty percent width benchmark,

00:34:00.205 --> 00:34:02.705
not a validated exclusion at this width.

00:34:04.002 --> 00:34:07.777
Below the plots, the top-pair mass spectrum
is computed with the interference

00:34:07.777 --> 00:34:11.822
between the Kaluza Klein gluon and the Q C
D gluon, in the fifteen bins of

00:34:11.822 --> 00:34:14.347
the C M S parton-level measurement.

00:34:14.347 --> 00:34:18.012
For this point the light-quark and top
couplings have opposite signs, so

00:34:18.012 --> 00:34:20.627
the interference is constructive below the
pole.

00:34:21.935 --> 00:34:26.740
Now load the flat G H U coloron at four
point five tera electron volts.

00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:30.920
Every quark couples equally, the couplings
share one sign, and the interference

00:34:30.920 --> 00:34:34.380
turns destructive: the low bins go down
instead of up.

00:34:35.668 --> 00:34:39.678
The last plot is the expected delta chi
squared against the measured covariance,

00:34:39.678 --> 00:34:42.823
with ten percent S M theory uncertainty per
bin.

00:34:42.823 --> 00:34:46.003
Set that uncertainty to zero and the reach
moves from about three point eight

00:34:46.003 --> 00:34:48.663
to about five tera electron volts.

00:34:48.663 --> 00:34:52.698
It is a sensitivity if the data equal the S
M, not an exclusion, and the

00:34:52.698 --> 00:34:57.228
open certificates show the independent
Dirac-trace and L H A P D F check.

00:35:00.335 --> 00:35:03.595
Use a comparison snapshot before changing a
point.

00:35:03.595 --> 00:35:08.985
Save the experiment JSON to retain the
inputs, calculated arrays, assumptions,

00:35:08.985 --> 00:35:11.570
provenance and optional saved reference.

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:15.020
The global result card and LaTeX exports
provide another view of the selected

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:16.330
calculation.

00:35:17.668 --> 00:35:20.463
Choose a figure and save its vector SVG.

00:35:20.463 --> 00:35:24.643
The new neutrino figures carry sampled data
and provenance in their metadata.

00:35:24.643 --> 00:35:28.333
The link button preserves controls in the
address; archive external results

00:35:28.333 --> 00:35:30.913
and comparisons in JSON as well.

00:35:32.202 --> 00:35:35.897
The browser calculations and stored
references run offline.

00:35:35.897 --> 00:35:39.922
New HiggsTools and PhaseTracer points use
the optional local engine.

00:35:39.922 --> 00:35:43.927
The source repository contains the
reproduction commands, independent checks

00:35:43.927 --> 00:35:48.507
and archived studies. Return to a chapter,
vary one assumption, and

00:35:48.507 --> 00:35:50.762
keep the scope beside every result.
